 The parabolas $y = (x + 1)^2$ and $x + 4 = (y - 3)^2$ have common points, since they are the graphs of functions. Each of these points is defined uniquely by its $x$-coordinate. Thus, evaluating both functions for the same value of $x$ gives the $y$-coordinate of their common point. More precisely, if \[(x,y) = (x,(x+1)^2)\]is a common point of $\{y=(x+1)^2\}$ and $\{x+4=(y-3)^2\},$ then \[\begin{align*}
x+4 &= \big((x+1)^2-3\big)^2\\
&= (x+1)^4-6(x+1)^2+9\\
&= x^4+2x^3+1\end{align*}\]and \[\begin{align*}
y &= (x+1)^2\\
&= x^2+2x+1.\end{align*}\]Equating the last two equations, we obtain \[x^4+2x^3+1 = (x^2+2x+1)^2 = x^4+4x^3+6x^2+4x+1\]which, after canceling identical terms and expanding, becomes \[0 = 2x^3+6x^2+4x = 2x(x+1)(x+2).\]The solutions are thus $x = 0,\>-1,$ and $-2.$ Substituting gives $$(0,1),\,(-1,0),\,\text{and}\,(-2,1)\, \text{as the common points of} \,y = (x + 1)^2 \,\text{and}\, x+4 = (y - 3)^2.$$<</think> <answer> $$9$$ </answer>

